<article class="env-lemma" id="0357">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0357">Lemma <span data-tag="0357">28.7.5</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a scheme such that any quasi-compact open has a finite number of irreducible components. The following are equivalent: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$X$ is normal, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$X$ is a disjoint union of normal integral schemes. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      It is immediate from the definitions that (2) implies (1). Let $X$ be a normal scheme such that every quasi-compact open has a finite number of irreducible components. If $X$ is affine then $X$ satisfies (2) by Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/030C" data-tag="030C">10.37.16</a>. For a general $X$, let $X = \bigcup X_ i$ be an affine open covering. Note that also each $X_ i$ has but a finite number of irreducible components, and the lemma holds for each $X_ i$. Let $T \subset X$ be an irreducible component. By the affine case each intersection $T \cap X_ i$ is open in $X_ i$ and an integral normal scheme. Hence $T \subset X$ is open, and an integral normal scheme. This proves that $X$ is the disjoint union of its irreducible components, which are integral normal schemes. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>